id: design.semaphore
kind: design
title: Semaphore
aliases: semaphore, permit, concurrency limit
tags: concurrency, reliability
questions:
  - how do you limit concurrency to a shared resource?
invariant: A semaphore issues a fixed number of permits; acquisition gates entry, release is guaranteed by scope.
answer_30s: A semaphore caps how many operations use a resource at once, so the limit is enforced where the resource is, not where the requests arrive.
answer_2m: |
  Distinguish it from a queue: a queue stores work, a semaphore limits active
  work. The two compose -- bounded queue upstream, semaphore around the resource
  -- and that pair is the worker-pool shape. Two patterns worth naming:
  acquire-then-work, where waiting is acceptable (in-flight limit per dependency),
  and try-acquire, where a full limit means shedding this request immediately
  (admission control). The second is the primitive that makes load shedding
  cheap and explicit instead of a timeout that fires too late.
when_to_use:
  - capping concurrent calls to a database, an upstream API, or a thread pool
avoid_when:
  - mutual exclusion (a lock) or handing work to another task (a queue)
minimal_example: |
  # conceptual: three permits, three concurrent callers, the fourth waits
  permits = Semaphore(3)
  with permits.acquire():   # or try_acquire() to shed instead of waiting
      call_upstream()
failure_modes:
  - permits released only on the success path, leaking the limit over time
  - a limit that is not derived from downstream capacity, so it protects nothing
related:
  - rust.tokio-semaphore
  - design.worker-pool
  - design.backpressure
  - design.load-shedding
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/sync/struct.Semaphore.html
